## 1 Introduction
Ammonia $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\right)$ is of critical importance to modern industry and agriculture and is used on an enormous scale worldwide. Given the high demand for $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ , considerable effort has been devoted to making its synthesis efficient, affordable, and environmentally sustainable [1]. The industrial production of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ relies upon the traditional Haber-Bosch method which is expensive with respect to plant equipment and has high energy demands which are commonly met by the combustion of fossil fuels [2,3]. With the aim of developing more sustainable processes, there has been considerable interest in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) for the production of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ because it can proceed under ambient conditions and consumes far less energy than the HaberBosch process [4–8]. However, the NRR process is usually accompanied by the competitive hydrogen evolution reaction (HER), resulting in a low efficiency for the NRR that can be attributed to multiple electron transfer processes and low $\Nu_{2}$ saturation in aqueous solution (generally not larger than $15\%$ ) [9,10]. As a result, considerable effort has been devoted to improving the activity of NRR electrocatalysts [11]. Some strategies have been proposed to optimize the intrinsic or apparent properties of catalysts [12]. Electrocatalysts employed to date for the NRR include noble-metal [13–15], non-noble-metal [16–20], and non-metal [21] materials.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a kind of porous materials composed of metal ions/clusters and organic ligands, which have become a hot topic in inorganic chemistry and material science. MOF materials with large specific surface areas, abundant unsaturated metal sites, and flexible topological structures are currently considered as ideal materials for adsorption [22,23], supercapacitor [24], fluorescence [25], sensing [26], and catalysis such as HER [27], oxygen evolution reaction (OER) [28], oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) [29], and NRR [30]. Although the application of MOFs in electrocatalysis is still in the early stage of research, it does not affect its enormous potential in the field of NRR. Some well-known MOFs, such as HKUST-1(Cu) [31], ZIF-67(Co) (ZIF $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ zeolitic imidazolate frameworks) [32], and MIL-100(Fe) [32] have been found to exhibit electrocatalytic activity with respect to the NRR. In biological systems, the Fe-based nitrogenase enzyme allows $\Nu_{2}$ fixation [33], and so inspired by nature, researchers have investigated Fe compounds in regard to ${\bf N}_{2}$ fixation. Indeed, theoretical calculations support the high electrocatalytic activity of such compounds in NRR [34,35]. Given the interest in both MOFs and Fe compounds, it is unsurprising that FeMOFs should receive particular attention with respect to the NRR [32,36–38]. However, challenges exist with respect to the activity and stability of such electrocatalysts. It is known that higher intrinsic conductivity and better access to active sites can be achieved by decreasing the size of MOF particles to nanoscale dimensions [39,40]. Using nano-sized particles is not without its disadvantages because if they become too small then the stability and reusability of the catalytic material can be compromised. One effective strategy that circumvents the problem associated with small particle size is to anchor the nano-sized MOF particles to a conductive support layer [41,42]. Amorphous ${\bf M o S}_{3}$ has attracted considerable attention as a result of its specific surface area and good electrical conductivity [43]. Furthermore, surface defects provide an abundance of sites for capturing and activating inert $\Nu_{2}$ molecules [44]. If a conductive ${\bf M o S}_{3}$ surface has Fe-MOF nano-sized particles bound to it, then it may prove to be an efficient and robust electrocatalytic material for the NRR.
Herein, we describe the conversion of micron-sized MIL101(Fe) octahedrons into nanodots, which are dispersed and bound to amorphous $\mathbf{MoS}_{3}$ nanosheets (referred to as MIL$101(\mathrm{Fe})/\mathrm{MoS}_{3})$ . The as-synthesized composite exhibits excellent electrocatalytic activity and durability for the NRR under acidic conditions. The Faraday efficiency of $36.71\%$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ yield of $25.7~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{mg_{\mathrm{cat}}}^{-1}$ at $-0.1\mathrm{~V~}\nu s$ . RHE in $0.1\mathrm{~M~HCl}$ , are superior to many other NRR catalysts. The high performance of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Fe})/\mathrm{MoS}_{3}$ as an electrocatalytic material is attributed to a synergistic effect between the MIL-101(Fe) nanodots and $\mathbf{MoS}_{3}$ nanosheets.
## 2 Results and discussion
The synthesis of the $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Fe})/\mathrm{MoS}_{3}$ composite via a precursor-transformation strategy is indicated in Scheme 1, and its detailed procedure is described in the Supporting Information online. MIL-101(Fe) was prepared by the combination of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ with terephthalate leading to the generation of the three-dimensional (3D) network material in which terephthalate anions bridge Fe(III) centres (Figure S1, Supporting Information online). The resulting MIL-101(Fe) crystals possess a uniform octahedral morphology with concave triangular faces and an edge length of about $500\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S2). The specific surface area of MIL-101(Fe) by the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) method is $2{,}760\mathrm{\m}^{2}\mathrm{\g}^{-1}$ (Figure S3), which indicates the high porosity of MIL-101 (Fe). When the MIL-101(Fe) crystals are combined with $\mathrm{(NH_{4})_{2}M o S_{4}}$ in DMF under solvothermal conditions, the MIL-101(Fe) octahedra undergo fragmentation to form ultrasmall particles (nanodots), while $\mathrm{(NH}_{4})_{2}\mathrm{MoS}_{4}$ is transformed into amorphous $\mathbf{MoS}_{3}$ nanosheets, thus yielding the MIL-101(Fe)/MoS3 nanocomposite. Notably, the size and morphology of the MIL-101(Fe) experienced a remarkable change under temperature stimulation. Although the mechanism of such morphology change is not understood for the time being, it is proposed that the coordination interaction between $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ and terephthalate ligands in the octahedral crystals of MIL-101 precursor may be partially broken under relatively high temperature solvothermal conditions. This makes the octahedral nanocrystals collapsed to form tiny nanodots, which are further anchored and stabilized by $\mathbf{MoS}_{3}$ nanosheets via the coordination interaction between $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ and ${\bf M o S}_{3}$ , thereby affording $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Fe})/\mathrm{MoS}_{3}$ composite nanosheets.
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images of the MIL$101(\mathrm{Fe})/\mathrm{MoS}_{3}$ composite indicate a uniformly dispersed sheet morphology with a course surface. Nanoparticles are clearly apparent on the ${\bf M o S}_{3}$ sheet surface (Figure 1a, b and Figure S4). Transmission electron microscope (TEM) images also reveal that the $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Fe})/\mathrm{MoS}_{3}$ nanosheet surface is rough with an average diameter of approximately $140\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 1c). A weak and broad peak around $8.6^{\circ}$ in the powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of MIL-101 $\mathrm{(Fe)}/\mathrm{MoS}_{3}$ can be attributed to the presence of MIL-101(Fe) (Figure 1d). The inferior quality of the powder diffraction pattern compared with that obtained on the precursor materials may be attributed to the relatively small size of the MIL101(Fe) nanodots and their dispersion across the ${\bf M o S}_{3}$ surface. The infrared spectrum of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Fe})/\mathrm{MoS}_{3}$ closely matches that of MIL-101(Fe) and $\mathrm{MoS}_{3}$ , while a broad band at $522~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ is attributed to the Mo-S stretching vibration of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MoS}}_{3}$ (Figure 1e). Broad bands at 215, 287 and $350~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in the Raman spectrum match those for pure $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MoS}}_{3}$ [43,45] (Figure 1f), and indicate the presence of $\mathrm{MoS}_{3}$ in the composite material.
A high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) image (Figure 1g) indicates only limited short-range stacking of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MoS}}_{3}$ sheets while the highly disordered lattice fringe showed the amorphous characteristics of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MoS}}_{3}$ in the composite material. In the regions outlined by red dashed circles, the lattice fringe with a $d$ spacing of $0.22{\mathrm{nm}}$ is consistent with good crystallinity for the MIL-101(Fe) nanodots [46]. This is confirmed by electron diffraction (SAED) patterns of the selected area (Figure 1h).
In addition to showing the presence of Mo and S, highangle annular dark field scanning TEM (HAADF-STEM) elemental mapping images (Figure 1i–j) indicate that Fe and O are uniformly distributed throughout the selected area of the $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Fe})/\mathrm{MoS}_{3}$ composite material. This provides further evidence for the even and widespread dispersion of the MIL-101(Fe) nanodots. The $\mathrm{Fe/Mo}$ mole ratio was determined to be 4:3 by SEM-energy dispersive spectrometer (EDS) (Figure S5), which is consistent with the inductively coupled plasma atomic emission spectrometry (ICP-AES) and elemental analysis (Table S1, Supporting Information online).
The elemental composition of the composite surface was investigated by X-ray photoelectron spectrometry (XPS). The full spectrum of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Fe})/\mathrm{MoS}_{3}$ reveals the presence of Fe, Mo, S and O (Figure 2a). The XPS patterns of Mo 3d, Fe 2p, S 2p are presented in Figure $2{\mathsf{b}}{\mathsf{-d}}$ . In the case of the $\mathrm{Mo}\ 3\mathrm{d}$ spectrum, two typical binding energies located at 231.9 and $228.7\mathrm{eV}$ are assigned to Mo $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ and Mo $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ , respectively, while the peak at $235.4\mathrm{eV}$ may be attributed to $\mathrm{Mo}^{6+}(\mathrm{O}_{3})$ (Figure 2b) [47]. The $\mathtt{S2p}$ spectrum has two peaks at 162.8 and $161.6\mathrm{eV},$ , corresponding to $\mathrm{~S~}2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ and S $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ , respectively. The doublet peak at 163.9 and $162.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ is consistent with the presence of bridging $\mathrm{S}^{2-}$ and/or apical $\mathrm{S}^{2-}$ ligands (Figure 2c) [48]. Comparison with the XPS spectrum of $\mathrm{MoS}_{3}$ confirms the successful conversion of the precursor $\mathrm{(NH}_{4})_{2}\mathrm{MoS}_{4}$ to ${\bf M o S}_{3}$ under solvothermal conditions (Figure S6).
The Fe 2p peaks located at 725.2 and $711.4\mathrm{eV}$ correspond to Fe $2{\mathsf{p}}_{1/2}$ and Fe $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ species, which is consistent with those in the Fe 2p spectra of the MIL-101(Fe) (Figure S7). The peak observed at $707.9\mathrm{eV}$ is mainly due to the coordination interaction of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ with $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MoS}}_{3}$ (Figure 3d). All these results strongly support the successful synthesis of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Fe})/\mathrm{MoS}_{3}$ composite material. Nitrogen adsorption-desorption measurements indicate a specific surface area $(85.5\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ for $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Fe})/\mathrm{MoS}_{3}$ . This is approximately three times greater than that expected for ${\bf M o S}_{3}$ nanosheets and can be attributed to the availability of additional binding sites arising from the presence of MIL-101(Fe) (Figure S8).
The electrochemical nitrogen reduction performance of the $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Fe})/\mathrm{MoS}_{3}$ catalyst was examined in an H-type electrolytic cell containing 0.1 M HCl. The initial investigation involved the comparison of an $\Nu_{2}$ saturated solution with an Ar saturated solution. To ensure gas saturation of the solution, a continuous stream of either $\Nu_{2}$ or Ar was allowed to flow into the cathode chamber at a rate of $60\mathrm{\mL\min}^{-1}$ for $30\mathrm{min}$ . Linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves, presented in Figure 3a, show the current density for the saturated $\Nu_{2}$ sample was higher than that for the saturated Ar sample, indicating the efficient initiation of the electrocatalytic $\Nu_{2}$ reduction process. In order to further investigate the catalytic performance of MIL- $101(\mathrm{Fe})/\mathrm{MoS}_{3}$ , electrolysis was continued at different applied potentials for $2\mathrm{h}$ . The yield of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ produced was monitored using the indophenol blue method [49], details of which are included in the Supporting Information online. The ultraviolet-visible (UV-Vis) absorption spectra at different $\mathrm{NH_{4}^{+}}$ concentrations are presented in Figure S9a. A calibration graph was prepared by plotting the absorbance of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ at $655\mathrm{nm}$ against the $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ concentration of the solution (Figure S9b). The relationship between $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ yield, faraday efficiency and different applied potential can be obtained using the calibration graph and relevant formulae. The UV-Vis absorption spectra recorded after $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ of electrolysis at applied potentials ranging from $-0.20$ to $0\mathrm{v}$ vs. RHE are presented in Figure 3b. Based on the calibration graph and chronoamperometry curve at different potentials (Figure S10), the absorbance at $-0.1\mathrm{~V~}\nu s$ . RHE is clearly superior to those obtained at other potentials. The $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ yield reached $25.7~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{mg_{\mathrm{cat}}}^{-1}$ with a faradaic efficiency (FE) of $36.71\%$ (Figure 3c), indicating that MIL$101(\mathrm{Fe})/\mathrm{MoS}_{3}$ has excellent NRR performance at $-0.1\mathrm{~V~}\nu s$ . RHE in $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ HCl. According to Table S2, such an electrocatalytic performance is close to those of some known electrocatalysts like Ru NNs [50] and NPG@ZIF-8 [51], but better than those of Au- $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ [52], Au nanorods [7], and $\mathrm{Pt}_{3}\mathrm{Fe}$ [53]. For comparative purposes, the NRR performance using pure MIL-101(Fe) or pure ${\bf M o S}_{3}$ was also investigated. After electrolysis at $-0.1\mathrm{V}$ vs. RHE for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ , both MIL-101 (Fe) and $\mathbf{MoS}_{3}$ showed lower $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ yields and faraday efficiencies than the $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Fe})/\mathrm{MoS}_{3}$ composite. The $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ yields were $9.38~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{mg_{\mathrm{cat}}}^{-1}$ (MIL-101(Fe)) and $\dot{0}.75\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{mg_{\mathrm{cat}}}^{-1}$ $(\mathrm{MoS}_{3})$ with FE of $13.39\%$ (MIL-101 (Fe)) and $0.03\%$ $(\mathrm{MoS}_{3})$ at the same potential, respectively (Figure 3d, e and Figures S11, S12). It is clear that the $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MoS}}_{3}$ surface decorated with MIL-101(Fe) nanodots is an effective material in promoting the conversion of $\Nu_{2}$ to $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ .
Isotope labeling experiments were also undertaken to confirm the source of nitrogen in $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ (Figure 3f). The $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ nuclear magnetic resonance $\mathrm{^{1}H_{\ I M R}})$ signal of $^{14}\mathrm{N/H_{4}}^{+}$ produces a triplet peak coupled with $^{14}\mathrm{N}$ in the region near $6.8–7.05\mathrm{ppm}$ , while that of $^{15}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{4}^{+}$ is split by the nuclear spin of $^{15}\mathrm{N}$ into a well-known double coupling peak in the same region, which is similar to those reported in the literatures [54,55]. When $^{15}\mathrm{N}_{2}$ and $^{14}\mathrm{N}_{2}$ were used as nitrogen sources, NMR signals $\left(\mathrm{DD}2\mathrm{-}600\mathrm{MHz}\right)$ indicating $^{15}\mathrm{{N}\bar{H}_{4}^{~+}}$ and NH4 respectively are apparent. By contrast, no response signal was found in the NMR test when Ar is used as the feed gas (Figure S13). These results indicate that the generated $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ is mainly derived from the reduction of ${\bf N}_{2}$ .
Comparative experiments were performed with (1) an Arsaturated solution at $-0.1\mathrm{~V~}\nu s$ . RHE, (2) an open circuit and (3) electrolysis of an $\Nu_{2}$ -saturated solution at $-0.1\mathrm{~V~}\nu s$ . RHE using carbon support. In each of these three cases, UV-Vis absorption peaks were clearly lower than that in an $\mathrm{N}_{2}.$ - saturated solution at $-0.1\mathrm{V}$ vs. RHE when the MIL-101(Fe)/ $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MoS}}_{3}$ composite was employed (Figure 4a).
In addition to the conversion of $\Nu_{2}$ to $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ during the electrochemical process, $\Nu_{2}$ reduction can also result in the generation of $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ . In order to evaluate the selectivity of the electrocatalyst in generating $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ , the classic Watt and Chrisp method was employed to detect the level of ${\mathrm{N}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}$ upon electrolysis of the electrolyte for $2\mathrm{h}$ at the same applied potentials [56]. A calibration graph was prepared by measuring the absorbances of hydrazine solutions of various known concentrations at $455\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S14a, b). Upon measuring the absorbance at $455\mathrm{nm}$ and referring to the calibration graph, it is apparent that after $2\mathrm{h}$ of electrolysis at $-0.1\mathrm{V}$ vs. RHE (with MIL-101(Fe)/MoS3 as the catalyst), almost no hydrazine is present. The results are the same after five cycles of experiments, proving the high selectivity of I $\mathsf{M I L-}101(\mathrm{Fe})/\mathrm{MoS}_{3}$ for NRR (Figure 4b).
The stability of the MIL- $101(\mathrm{Fe})/\mathrm{MoS}_{3}$ composite during the $\Nu_{2}$ reduction process was also investigated. The absorbance (Figure S15), current density (Figure S16), and $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ yield faraday efficiency (Figure 4c) did not show any significant deterioration in performance after five consecutive electrolysis runs of $\Nu_{2}$ using $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Fe})/\mathrm{MoS}_{3}$ at $-0.1\mathrm{V}$ vs. RHE, and the MIL- $101/\mathrm{MoS}_{3}$ nanohybrid after electrocatalysis for NRR still maintained almost the same morphology (Figure S17). In addition, the current attenuation at $-0.1\mathrm{~V~}\nu s$ . RHE under continuous electrolysis for $12\mathrm{h}$ was also negligible (Figure 4d). The experimental results support good catalytic stability with respect to the MIL-101(Fe)/ $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MoS}}_{3}$ composite. The XPS spectra of Mo 3d (Figure 4e) recorded before and after the NRR indicate no significant shift; however a shift was noted in the Fe 2p spectrum (Figure 4f) which is consistent with the Fe centers participating in electron transfer during the NRR process.
## 3 Conclusions
In summary, the $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Fe})/\mathrm{MoS}_{3}$ hybrid composite has been prepared by a simple precursor-transformation strategy. The composite material, consisting of MIL-101(Fe) nanodots decorated on $\mathbf{MoS}_{3}$ nanosheets, has been shown to exhibit efficient electrocatalytic behavior. The relatively high surface area of the composite material would appear to facilitate the adsorption of nitrogen. With the reduction in particle size, there is a substantial increase in the availability of catalytic active sites, which significantly enhances the electrocatalytic activity of the NRR. At $-0.1\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. RHE in acid solution, the $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ yield and the Faradaic efficiency extended up to $25.7~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{m{g_{\mathrm{cat}}}^{-1}}$ and $36.71\%$ , respectively. Compared with pure MIL-101(Fe) and $\mathrm{MoS}_{3}$ , this catalyst not only offers improved electrocatalytic performance, but also exhibits excellent selectivity and durability. The generation of a catalytically active hybrid material, formed by the dispersion of ultrasmall MIL-101(Fe) nanodots on amorphous $\mathbf{MoS}_{3}$ , offers considerable promise for the design and development of high performance electrocatalysts for $\Nu_{2}$ fixation.
